<TopicPage slug="subjects/public-administration/foundational/the-administrative-state">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Public administration is the study of how government actually runs — the agencies, budgets,
    rules and civil services that turn laws into permits, pensions, inspections and pavement. It
    lives in the gap between what politics promises and what organisations can deliver. A
    parliament can pass a clean-air law in an afternoon; making the air cleaner takes monitoring
    stations, emission standards, inspectors, courts and decades of budget lines. That machinery —
    permanent, professional, unelected — is called the <strong>administrative state</strong>, and
    modern government is mostly made of it. In most developed countries the civil service employs
    more people than the armed forces, and the regulatory agencies touch more daily life than any
    legislature ever votes on directly.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why does it need studying at all? Because it is a standing paradox. Democracy says power should
    belong to the people's elected representatives; administration says complex, technical,
    around-the-clock governance needs full-time specialists who cannot be re-elected out of
    expertise. Every modern state has answered the paradox the same way — delegate enormous power
    to bureaucracies, then build elaborate machinery to hold them accountable — and every serious
    question in this field is a variation on that theme: how much discretion should officials have?
    Who watches the watchers? How do you get a bureaucracy that is both competent and legitimate?
  </p>
  <p>
    This page works through the machinery itself (the administrative state and its bureaucratic
    design), the multi-level geometry of federal systems, and the increasingly blurred boundary
    where government hands work to non-profits and private firms. A closing section takes stock of
    where the debate now stands.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the machinery and its checks" widget="equationStep" config={adminCfg} />

  <h2>The administrative state: bureaucracy as a design</h2>
  <p>
    The founding problem was named by Woodrow Wilson in "The Study of Administration" (1887):
    how can government be run competently without corrupting democracy — and how can democracy
    steer it without smothering its competence? Wilson's proposed answer, the
    politics–administration dichotomy, held that elected politicians should decide <em>what</em> to
    do and neutral professionals should decide <em>how</em> to do it. The dichotomy has never
    survived contact with reality intact — every "how" is also a "what" — but the question it
    framed still organises the field. The American context made it urgent: the spoils system, in
    which each new administration staffed agencies with political loyalists, produced both
    corruption and incompetence, and the assassination of President Garfield in 1881 by a disappointed
    office-seeker pushed through the Pendleton Act (1883), which began the merit-based civil service:
    positions filled by examination, protection from political dismissal.
  </p>
  <p>
    The sociologist Max Weber gave the design its theory. His "ideal type" of bureaucracy is a
    hierarchy of offices with clear jurisdiction; written rules and records ("files"); officials
    appointed on merit and salaried, not elected or purchased; and career ladders that reward
    expertise. The point of all this is a specific promise: <strong>impartiality</strong>. A
    citizen should get the same permit, the same benefit, the same treatment regardless of who
    they know or what they paid — the rule, not the official's mood, decides. This is what
    distinguishes modern government from patronage machines, and it is why the least glamorous
    bureaucratic features — the forms, the queues, the documented procedures — are the technology
    of fairness.
  </p>
  <p>
    But the design has known costs, and "bureaucracy" became an insult for a reason. Rules are
    written for the typical case and mishandle the atypical one. Rule-following can displace
    results — an agency can perfectly comply with procedure while the actual problem goes unsolved.
    Hierarchy filters information on the way up and distortion on the way down. Officials become
    budget-maximisers and empire-builders (the public-choice critique); the accumulation of
    procedure-over-purpose earns its nickname, <strong>red tape</strong>. And most importantly,
    discretion never actually disappears: statutes are incomplete, so officials must fill gaps.
    Michael Lipsky's study of <strong>street-level bureaucrats</strong> — teachers, police officers,
    caseworkers, inspectors — showed that these frontline workers, facing more clients than time,
    effectively <em>make</em> policy in the patterns of their daily coping: which claims get
    expedited, which infractions ignored, which students pushed. Governance happens at the desk,
    not only in the chamber. The real design questions are therefore not "discretion or no
    discretion" but how much, guided by what expertise, under whose oversight.
  </p>
  <p>
    The answers to the last question form the accountability architecture. Agencies answer to
    <strong>elected executives</strong> (ministers, presidents, mayors) who appoint leadership;
    to <strong>legislatures</strong> through hearings, budget control and questioning; to
    <strong>courts</strong> through judicial review of whether rules were followed and discretion
    abused; and to specialised watchdogs — audit offices, inspectors general, ombudsmen,
    freedom-of-information laws, plus the media and interest groups. None of these checks is
    sufficient alone; each catches a different failure (courts catch illegality, auditors catch
    waste, elections catch unpopularity). Layered together they are the legitimacy engine of
    unelected power — the administrative state is defensible exactly to the degree that this
    layering works.
  </p>
  <p>
    Reform movements keep rewriting the answer. The mid-century confidence in expert agencies gave
    way in the 1980s–90s to the <strong>New Public Management</strong>: import private-sector
    techniques, set targets and performance indicators, contract out delivery, treat citizens as
    customers ("Reinventing Government", Osborne and Gaebler, 1992, became its manifesto). NPM
    bought measurable efficiency and paid for it in target-gaming, fragmentation and demoralised
    services — hospitals optimising a four-hour waiting metric while the deep problem worsened.
    Current waves — "New Public Governance", co-production, a renewed focus on equity, trust and
    digital government — are corrections in the other direction. Each generation rediscovers
    Weber's trade-off: rules buy fairness and cost flexibility; flexibility buys responsiveness
    and costs consistency.
  </p>

  <h2>Federalism and multi-level governance: who does what</h2>
  <p>
    Few services citizens actually meet are delivered by a single level of government. The
    characteristic arrangement of a federal (or quasi-federal) system is a <em>division of
    labour</em>: the national level sets standards, redistributes money and handles what must be
    uniform (defence, currency, cross-border matters); intermediate governments (states, provinces,
    Länder) legislate and administer broad domestic programmes; local governments deliver the
    day-to-day — schools, waste, local roads, housing permits — because proximity is itself an
    administrative virtue. The school your child attends is typically staffed and run locally,
    funded partly by the state, regulated and subsidised by the national government. Governance is
    a joint product; the question "which level is responsible?" usually has a three-part answer.
  </p>
  <p>
    The plumbing of this arrangement is the <strong>grants system</strong>. National money flows
    down as <strong>categorical grants</strong> (specific purpose, with conditions attached) or
    <strong>block grants</strong> (broad purpose, more local freedom). Conditions are the
    national government's steering wheel: it cannot always command a state to act, but it can
    attach requirements to money the state wants — which is how national standards in areas as
    diverse as highway safety and drinking water get implemented by subnational agencies. This
    model — shared money, shared administration, negotiated turf — is called
    <strong>cooperative federalism</strong>, and it replaced the older "layer cake" image of
    watertight jurisdictions.
  </p>
  <p>
    Why split the work at all? Two principles justify it. <strong>Subsidiarity</strong>: decisions
    should be made at the lowest level competent to make them, because local knowledge is real
    knowledge and local accountability is the sharpest. <strong>Uniformity</strong>: some goods —
    rights, environmental floors, a single market — must not vary by postcode, so the top level
    must set the floor. The tension between these two principles is where most federal political
    fights actually live. A worked example: clean air. A national agency sets ambient standards and
    emission limits (uniformity); state environmental agencies write the implementation plans,
    issue permits and run inspections (subsidiarity); a municipality runs transit and zoning that
    shape emissions on the ground; and when a state plan falls short, the national agency may
    withhold funding or impose a federal plan. One policy, three levels, constant negotiation.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> that federalism means "states do their own thing". In practice, most modern federalism is <em>intergovernmental administration</em> — national frameworks, subnational implementation, jointly financed — and the daily work of public administration happens in these multi-level programmes, not in clean constitutional silos. Similarly, "local government knows best" and "national standards protect everyone" are both true; the craft of the field is managing the trade-off, not picking a side.</p>

  <h2>The non-profit and private interfaces: the blurring state</h2>
  <p>
    The twentieth century ended with government <em>doing less and steering more</em>. Services
    once delivered by public employees are now frequently contracted to private firms (prisons,
    IT systems, waste collection, employment services), delivered by non-profit organisations
    under public funding (social housing associations, charities running employment programmes,
    church-affiliated hospitals), or produced jointly with citizens themselves. Political scientists
    call the resulting pattern the <strong>hollow state</strong>: the shell of public authority
    remains — the law, the budget, the accountability — while much of the delivery is performed by
    third parties. Non-profit delivery has deep roots (in many countries, charities and voluntary
    hospitals predate the welfare state and were folded into public funding rather than replaced),
    and the non-profit sector remains the government's most trusted delivery partner in areas like
    disaster relief, homelessness and international development.
  </p>
  <p>
    The arguments for the interfaces are real. Competition can lower costs; specialisation brings
    expertise a generalist agency cannot maintain; non-profits reach communities and earn trust in
    ways officials cannot; contracting lets government scale capacity up and down. The arguments
    against are equally real, and they are mostly <em>accountability</em> problems in disguise.
    A private contractor's incentive is profit; a non-profit's is mission and survival; neither is
    identical to the public interest, and the gap must be managed by the contract — an instrument
    that can specify what was bought but rarely what government actually needs until it changes.
    Contractors acquire expertise government loses ("we cannot replace them because only they
    understand the system they built"). Outsourced failures are politically blamed on government
    anyway, since citizens cannot see the corporate shell behind the service. And the classic
    Pressman and Wildavsky finding — that implementation fails in the <em>joint</em> action of many
    independent actors, each with veto points and separate priorities — is at its most severe when
    the actors are separate organisations in a network rather than units of one hierarchy.
  </p>
  <p>
    A worked miniature makes the management task concrete. A city contracts waste collection to a
    private firm and homelessness outreach to a non-profit. The waste contract can be specified —
    bins per street, missed-collection penalties, performance bonds — so competitive tendering
    plausibly works. The outreach contract cannot be specified that way ("trust established"
    resists measurement), so the city relies on relationship management, outcome reporting and the
    non-profit's own accountability to donors and clients. Smart administration therefore matches
    the instrument to the service: contracts for measurable, contestable work; grants and
    partnerships for relational, evolving work; in-house delivery where discretion must remain
    public and expertise must stay internal.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    Three deeper currents are worth following. First, the <strong>measurement problem</strong>: the
    New Public Management's promise of evidence-based accountability collided with the sociologist's
    law that when a measure becomes a target it ceases to be a good measure — waiting-time targets
    gamed by re-booking, crime statistics massaged, "teaching to the test". The modern synthesis
    treats metrics as one fallible input among several, to be triangulated with professional
    judgement and citizen feedback, rather than as the answer. Second, the <strong>principal–agent
    problem</strong> formalises all delegation: the principal (voters, ministers) cannot perfectly
    observe the agent (agency, contractor), so the design question is incentive and monitoring
    structures under asymmetric information — which is why audits, open data and FOI laws are not
    bureaucratic decoration but information-forcing devices. Third, <strong>trust and capacity</strong>:
    surveys across the OECD show declining trust in government, and the field increasingly argues
    that service experience is the main driver — people judge the state by the queue at the permit
    office, not by the macroeconomy. That reframes competence itself as the foundation of
    legitimacy: the administrative state is not merely machinery that democracy must tolerate, but
    the thing that makes democratic promises physically come true. Its reform debates — how to
    recruit and keep expertise, how to digitise without surveilling, how to buy agility without
    hollowing out — are, in the end, debates about whether modern democracies can still keep their
    word.
  </p>

